<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Lahej</h1><p class="st-meta">1728 CE – 1967 CE · capital Lahej</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Abdali sultanate north of Aden, which lost Aden to Britain in 1839 but remained the leading state of the Aden hinterland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Abdali tribe held the fertile Lahej oasis north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>. Around 1728, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fadl-ibn-ali">Fadl ibn Ali</a>, they rejected the authority of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qasimid-state">Qasimid State</a> and founded an independent sultanate, which also took control of the port of Aden.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lahej lived on irrigated agriculture and the trade passing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and the interior. In 1839 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a> seized Aden from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhsin-ibn-fadl">Muhsin ibn Fadl</a>, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aden-settlement">Aden Settlement</a>, and Lahej became the most important of the protected states around it. Ottoman forces occupied Lahej in 1915-1918. The sultanate's court was a centre of poetry and song, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-fadl-al-qumandan">Ahmad Fadl al-Qumandan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abd-al-karim">Ali ibn Abd al-Karim</a> in 1958 for his nationalist contacts. Lahej joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-south-arabia">Federation of South Arabia</a>, and in 1967 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> took over, abolishing the sultanate within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-lahej.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Lahej" width="320" height="161"><figcaption>Image: RsAzevedo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sultanate_of_Lahej.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1728 CE – 1967 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lahej</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fadl ibn Ali al-Abdali, Muhsin ibn Fadl, Ali ibn Abd al-Karim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Abdali Sultanate, Lahej, Lahij</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=sultanate-of-lahej" aria-label="Sultanate of Lahej on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5927 1690 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Lahej lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6247 1946L6236 1957M6247 1946L6207 1863M6247 1946L6295 1853M6247 1946L6313 1863M6247 1946L6313 1863M6247 1946L6236 1957"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6236 1957h0M6207 1863h0M6295 1853h0M6313 1863h0M6313 1863h0M6236 1957h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6247 1946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6247 1946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=sultanate-of-lahej">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
